1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
Text on screen: Vera Institute of Justice

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Vera Voices Podcast Series

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Neil A. Weiner, Research Speaker Series, presents: David Weisburd, PhD, Distinguished Professor George Mason University

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:15,000
and Director, Institute of Criminology, The Hebrew University

5
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Hot Spots of Crime and Crime Prevention with David Weisburd, PhD,

6
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Distinguished Professor George Mason University and Director, Institute of Criminology, The Hebrew University

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:27,000
Hot spots of crime are a new unit of analysis, if you like,

8
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,000
in trying to understand crime problems and do something about them.

9
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Most of the time criminologists or crime prevention specialists have thought about doing something about the crime problem,

10
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:40,000
they focus on two units: one, either the individual.

11
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Most of what we do about the crime problem, up to now at least, has been focused on individuals;

12
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:47,000
how we can stop them from recidivating.

13
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:55,000
But also on large macrounits of geography, like communities or neighborhoods or beats or precincts.

14
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Hot spots of crime refers to something very different, on a much smaller microgeographic level.

15
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:06,000
A hot spot can be a single street segment. It could be a small group of street segments.

16
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:13,000
But still very much smaller, in geographic terms, than traditional units of "places," if you like,

17
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:17,000
that have interested criminologists or others concerned about the crime problem.

18
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:20,000
The question of why they're important, one simple way of saying that,

19
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:25,000
is that we looked at the distribution of crime across geography.

20
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:31,000
We've found that crime is extraordinarily concentrated at small, microgeographic hot spots.

21
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:36,000
For example, in Seattle, Washington, over a 14-year period, for every year,

22
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:44,000
50 percent of the crime is found at less than 5 percent of the street segments in the city.

23
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,000
These findings have been replicated in a whole series of studies.

24
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:51,000
Crime is very concentrated at place.

25
00:01:51,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Also in Seattle, 86 street segments out of almost 30,000 have about a third of all juvenile crime incidents.

26
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:06,000
Well, this concentration of crime at these hot spots presents a great advantage.

27
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:12,000
We can deal with a lot of crime by dealing with a relatively few places in the city.

28
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:17,000
The question of why we should put more emphasis on crime hot spots has to do, in part,

29
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:20,000
with the issue of concentration that I noted already.

30
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:25,000
If a lot of crime is concentrated at a very small group of places,

31
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:29,000
we can have a big effect on the crime problem by concentrating on those places.

32
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:33,000
But it's not just that crime is concentrated at a small number of places.

33
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:39,000
When we look at the data, we find that crime is relatively stable over time at those places.

34
00:02:39,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Now, compare that to individual offending, where we know that people start out in their mid-teens and late teens,

35
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:51,000
committing crimes, then they go to relatively large rates of criminal activity in their early 20s.

36
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:54,000
And then they start to age out of crime.

37
00:02:54,000 --> 00:02:58,000
So, crime is relatively unstable across the life course for individuals.

38
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:04,000
We've looked at 14 years of data in Seattle, and what we've found is that 1 percent of the street segments

39
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:09,000
produce about 23 percent of the crime, and they stay hot throughout that period.

40
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:12,000
So, they're chronic hot spots. They're stable.

41
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:17,000
Now, I should note as well that these hot spots have characteristics that make them hot.

42
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:19,000
There are reasons why crime comes to those places.

43
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:24,000
Sometimes it's because it's a place where they; with a train stop or a bus stop

44
00:03:24,000 --> 00:03:28,000
or other places where people are getting on and off with lots of victims around.

45
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:33,000
We've found that, for example, where there are large groups of employees on a block there tends to be more crime.

46
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:37,000
Well, that also means we have a sense of why crime is occurring there,

47
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:40,000
but we can also try to develop strategies to do something about it.

48
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:44,000
There is a series of studies, many of them randomized experiments,

49
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:49,000
that show that if the police focus on crime hot spots, that crime will go down in those places.

50
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:52,000
Now, the simplest response to that is often,

51
00:03:52,000 --> 00:03:57,000
"Well, they might go down there, but won't you just push crime around the corner?"

52
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:02,000
Well, interestingly enough, the evidence we have says that's not gonna happen.

53
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:06,000
At least, you're not gonna have enough crime pushed around the corner

54
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:11,000
to make the crime prevention efforts carried out at those places not worthwhile.

55
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:17,000
In that group of studies that we know of, in which police have concentrated on crime hot spots,

56
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:21,000
we found not only that crime goes down at the hot spots,

57
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:27,000
but when the crime does change in the areas immediately surrounding, it doesn't go up. It goes down.

58
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:32,000
In other words, you don't have displacement of crime; you have what we call a diffusion of crime control benefits.

59
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:38,000
In other words, if you crack down on hot spots, more likely than not, the areas around the hot spots will get better.

60
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:44,000
Well, if that's the case, then certainly hot spots policing makes very good sense.

61
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:48,000
This is a strategy that can work, and can work without displacement.

62
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:53,000
And that's why the National Academy of Sciences, in their report on police practices and policies,

63
00:04:53,000 --> 00:04:59,000
concluded that the strongest evidence we have about effective policing is about hot spots policing.

64
00:04:59,000 --> 00:05:07,000
Up till now, most of the approaches that we've brought against hot spots have been law enforcement approaches

65
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,000
in which the police and police strategies have predominated.

66
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:16,000
But work we've been doing suggests that maybe we ought to think a bit more broadly.

67
00:05:16,000 --> 00:05:20,000
We've looked at the causes of criminal activity at these hot spots.

68
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:24,000
We often find that there are, let's call them social causes.

69
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:29,000
Or, at least they're causes that can be addressed not only by the police and;

70
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:34,000
but also by other criminal justice agencies and, perhaps, non-criminal justice agencies.

71
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,000
We've found that social characteristics of places

72
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:45,000
(poverty, elements of young people hanging out in the streets, etcetera)

73
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:48,000
that those have a strong effect on whether a place is a crime hot spot.

74
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:53,000
I think we might want to think in the long term about how we can change these small places

75
00:05:53,000 --> 00:06:03,000
so that the social features are less conducive to crime, including poverty, including family issues, children, etcetera.

76
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:08,000
I would say that the importance of this, and the importance of hot spots here, is that, for the most part,

77
00:06:08,000 --> 00:06:13,000
crime prevention specialists have said, "We can't do something about poverty in the whole city.

78
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:18,000
We can't change the nature of juvenile activities in the whole city."

79
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:24,000
But you know what? If there's a relatively small group of places that are hot spots, maybe we can change those places.

80
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:28,000
And maybe by changing those places, we can do something about the crime problem.

81
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:32,000
And if we take those kinds of approaches, by the way, we'll also do something else very important.

82
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:36,000
We imprison an enormous number of people in the United States.

83
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:39,000
Imprisonment comes from law enforcement.

84
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:43,000
When the police are around, one of the things they might do is arrest people.

85
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:45,000
They have a tendency towards law enforcement.

86
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:48,000
With that in mind, to the extent that we can deal with these hot spots without law enforcement,

87
00:06:48,000 --> 00:06:57,000
we can get crime prevention benefits without the disabilities or the problems associated with arrest and imprisonment.

88
00:06:57,000 --> 00:07:06,000
Vera Institute of Justice, Vera Voices Podcast Series

89
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:12,000
